              "name": "EKS cluster without control plane logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EKS cluster without control plane logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without enabled_cluster_log_types, EKS control-plane audit and authenticator logs are not sent to CloudWatch, blinding incident response.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-307",
              "name": "Lambda function env vars without KMS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function env vars without KMS"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An environment block with variables but no kms_key_arn leaves environment variables (often secrets) encrypted only with an AWS-owned key.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt Lambda environment variables with a customer-managed KMS key:\nresource \"aws_lambda_function\" \"this\" {\n  # ...\n  kms_key_arn = aws_kms_key.lambda.arn\n}\nBetter: don't put secrets in env vars at all — fetch from Secrets Manager at runtime."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-envvars-encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-308",
              "name": "Lambda without active X-Ray tracing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda without active X-Ray tracing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without tracing_config { mode = \"Active\" }, Lambda invocations are not traced in X-Ray, reducing observability of failures and latency.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-309",
              "name": "Deprecated launch configuration in use",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Deprecated launch configuration in use"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "aws_launch_configuration is deprecated by AWS; migrate to aws_launch_template, which receives new features and supports versioning.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-310",
              "name": "EC2 instance without detailed monitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without detailed monitoring"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With monitoring absent or false (the provider default), the instance ships only basic 5-minute CloudWatch metrics, delaying detection of performance and availability issues.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-311",
              "name": "RDS without IAM database authentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS without IAM database authentication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With iam_database_authentication_enabled absent or false, database access relies on long-lived passwords instead of short-lived IAM credentials, weakening credential hygiene.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-312",
              "name": "DynamoDB table without point-in-time recovery",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DynamoDB table without point-in-time recovery"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a point_in_time_recovery block, DynamoDB continuous backups are disabled and the table cannot be restored to an arbitrary point in the preceding 35 days.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-313",
              "name": "DynamoDB table without customer-managed encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DynamoDB table without customer-managed encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "AWS encrypts DynamoDB tables at rest by default with AWS-owned keys; this rule treats a missing server_side_encryption block as insufficient because AWS-owned keys offer no customer control over key rotation or revocation. Set server_side_encryption { enabled = true, kms_key_arn = ... }.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-314",
              "name": "Redshift cluster unencrypted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redshift cluster unencrypted"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Redshift clusters are unencrypted unless encrypted = true; unencrypted clusters expose data at rest on cluster storage.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-315",
              "name": "Redshift cluster publicly accessible",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redshift cluster publicly accessible"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A publicly_accessible Redshift cluster accepts connections from the internet, exposing the data warehouse to network attacks.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-317",
              "name": "EFS file system unencrypted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EFS file system unencrypted"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "EFS file systems are unencrypted unless encrypted = true; unencrypted file systems expose data at rest.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-323",
              "name": "Load balancer without deletion protection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer without deletion protection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without enable_deletion_protection, a load balancer can be destroyed by a single API call or Terraform change, taking the service offline. The provider default is disabled.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-324",
              "name": "Load balancer listener with weak TLS policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer listener with weak TLS policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A TLS listener using a pre-TLS-1.2 security policy (e.g. ELBSecurityPolicy-2016-08) negotiates weak cipher suites. Use a TLS 1.2+ policy such as ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-325",
              "name": "CloudFront distribution allows HTTP",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudFront distribution allows HTTP"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A cache behavior with viewer_protocol_policy = \"allow-all\" serves content over unencrypted HTTP. Use \"https-only\" or \"redirect-to-https\".\n\nHow to fix:\nRedirect HTTP to HTTPS on the CloudFront distribution:\nresource \"aws_cloudfront_distribution\" \"this\" {\n  # ...\n  default_cache_behavior {\n    viewer_protocol_policy = \"redirect-to-https\"\n    # ...\n  }\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/using-https-viewers-to-cloudfront.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-326",
              "name": "API Gateway custom domain with weak TLS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "API Gateway custom domain with weak TLS"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A custom domain name with security_policy below TLS_1_2 (e.g. TLS_1_0) negotiates weak cipher suites. The modern provider default is TLS_1_2.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnforce TLS 1.2+. Replace HTTP listeners with HTTPS, set a modern TLS policy (e.g. `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06`), and redirect HTTP→HTTPS. Disable weak ciphers and old protocol versions everywhere."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/elb-security-policy-table.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-327",
              "name": "API Gateway stage without access logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "API Gateway stage without access logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A stage without an access_log_settings block leaves no audit trail of API invocations, hampering incident response and anomaly detection.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-328",
              "name": "Network ACL with open ingress",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Network ACL with open ingress"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An inline NACL ingress rule allowing 0.0.0.0/0 exposes the subnet to the internet at the stateless network layer. NACLs are a coarse control; restrict ingress to known ranges.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-329",
              "name": "Load balancer target group uses plain HTTP",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer target group uses plain HTTP"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A target group with protocol = \"HTTP\" forwards traffic to targets unencrypted, exposing internal traffic on the network. Use HTTPS for backend connections.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnforce TLS 1.2+. Replace HTTP listeners with HTTPS, set a modern TLS policy (e.g. `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06`), and redirect HTTP→HTTPS. Disable weak ciphers and old protocol versions everywhere."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/elb-security-policy-table.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-330",
              "name": "CloudFront distribution without WAF",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudFront distribution without WAF"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A distribution with no web_acl_id attached has no WAFv2 protection against common web exploits and bots at the edge.\n\nHow to fix:\nAttach a WAFv2 Web ACL with managed rule groups (AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet,\nAWSManagedRulesKnownBadInputsRuleSet) to the load balancer / API / distribution.\nSet the default action to block and monitor via CloudWatch metrics."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-chapter.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-331",
              "name": "Network ACL rule with open ingress",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Network ACL rule with open ingress"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A standalone aws_network_acl_rule allowing 0.0.0.0/0 ingress exposes the subnet to the internet at the stateless network layer. Restrict ingress to known ranges.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-332",
              "name": "API Gateway v2 stage without access logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "API Gateway v2 stage without access logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A v2 (HTTP/WebSocket) stage without an access_log_settings block leaves no audit trail of API invocations, hampering incident response and anomaly detection.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-333",
              "name": "Inline admin policy attached to role/user/group",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Inline admin policy attached to role/user/group"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An inline IAM policy with an Allow statement granting Action \"*\" (or \"*:*\") on Resource \"*\" is administrative access. Inline policies are harder to audit than managed ones; scope them to least privilege.\n\nHow to fix:\nMove the inline admin policy to a scoped customer-managed policy (see\nAEGIS-AWS-006 fix). Inline policies on identities are hard to audit — prefer\nmanaged policies attached to groups/roles."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html#grant-least-privilege",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-335",
              "name": "KMS key without rotation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "KMS key without rotation"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Customer-managed KMS keys should rotate annually. enable_key_rotation defaults to false. (AWS-managed keys rotate automatically and cannot set this flag; a narrow false positive is possible if an AWS-managed key is declared explicitly.)\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable automatic annual rotation on the KMS key:\nresource \"aws_kms_key\" \"this\" {\n  enable_key_rotation = true\n  # ...\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/rotate-keys.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-336",
              "name": "CloudTrail not multi-region",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudTrail not multi-region"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A single-region trail misses API activity in every other region. is_multi_region_trail defaults to false.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable CloudTrail in all regions with log file validation:\nresource \"aws_cloudtrail\" \"this\" {\n  is_multi_region_trail         = true\n  enable_log_file_validation    = true\n  kms_key_id                    = aws_kms_key.cloudtrail.arn\n  s3_bucket_name                = aws_s3_bucket.trail.id\n  include_global_service_events = true\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-337",
              "name": "Secrets Manager rotation interval too long",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager rotation interval too long"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Secrets Manager rotation interval above 90 days keeps a compromised credential usable for too long. Rotation cadence is configured on aws_secretsmanager_secret_rotation via rotation_rules.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-338",
              "name": "SSM parameter stores plaintext secret",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSM parameter stores plaintext secret"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An SSM parameter whose name suggests a secret but whose type is plain String (not SecureString) stores the value unencrypted. The value itself is never included in findings; it is redacted by the report layer.\n\nHow to fix:\nConvert the parameter to SecureString with a KMS key:\nresource \"aws_ssm_parameter\" \"this\" {\n  type   = \"SecureString\"\n  key_id = aws_kms_key.ssm.arn\n  value  = var.secret_value  # never hardcode\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/systems-manager-parameter-store.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-339",
              "name": "Hardcoded credentials in resource config",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded credentials in resource config"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A database password written as a literal in Terraform config is stored in state, plan output, and version control. The same holds for a secret-like variable or local flowing into a credential attribute. Reference a secrets manager or a generated password instead. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded credential and rotate it immediately — it is compromised.\nReference a Secrets Manager secret instead:\ndata \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"db\" {\n  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db.id\n}\n# then: password = jsondecode(data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db.secret_string)[\"password\"]\nIf the value was committed to git, purge it from history (BFG/truffleHog) and rotate."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-341",
              "name": "SNS topic without KMS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SNS topic without KMS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "SNS topics should encrypt messages at rest with a KMS key. A topic with no kms_master_key_id relies on unencrypted delivery.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt the SNS topic with a customer-managed KMS key:\nresource \"aws_sns_topic\" \"this\" {\n  kms_master_key_id = aws_kms_key.sns.arn\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-server-side-encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-342",
              "name": "SQS queue without encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQS queue without encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "SQS queues should encrypt messages at rest (SSE-SQS via sqs_managed_sse_enabled or SSE-KMS via kms_master_key_id). A queue with SSE-SQS explicitly disabled and no KMS key stores plaintext. Note: modern provider defaults enable SSE-SQS, so an absent sqs_managed_sse_enabled is treated as enabled (no finding).\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt the SQS queue with a customer-managed KMS key:\nresource \"aws_sqs_queue\" \"this\" {\n  kms_master_key_id = aws_kms_key.sqs.arn\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSSimpleQueueService/latest/SQSDeveloperGuide/sqs-server-side-encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-343",
              "name": "SNS topic policy allows public principal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SNS topic policy allows public principal"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A topic policy Allow statement with Principal \"*\" grants anonymous publish/subscribe access to the topic.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-344",
              "name": "SQS queue policy allows public principal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQS queue policy allows public principal"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A queue policy Allow statement with Principal \"*\" grants anonymous send/receive access to the queue.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-347",
              "name": "CloudWatch log group without retention",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudWatch log group without retention"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "CloudWatch log groups should set retention_in_days so logs expire on schedule. A group with no retention keeps logs forever, growing cost and breach blast radius.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-348",
              "name": "GuardDuty detector disabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GuardDuty detector disabled"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "GuardDuty is the account's threat-detection baseline. A detector with enable = false leaves the account without anomaly detection.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-351",
              "name": "DocumentDB cluster unencrypted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DocumentDB cluster unencrypted"
              },
              "help": {
