                "text": "A DynamoDB table without deletion_protection_enabled can be deleted by anyone with table-management permissions.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-419",
              "name": "Athena workgroup configuration not enforced",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Athena workgroup configuration not enforced"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With enforce_workgroup_configuration = false, queries can override the workgroup's encryption and output settings, bypassing its controls.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-420",
              "name": "KMS key policy allows public principal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "KMS key policy allows public principal"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An Allow statement with Principal \"*\" in a KMS key policy lets any AWS principal use the key.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-421",
              "name": "Cognito identity pool allows unauthenticated identities",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cognito identity pool allows unauthenticated identities"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An identity pool that grants access to unauthenticated users hands AWS credentials to anyone on the internet. Disable allow_unauthenticated_identities unless guest access is intended.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-422",
              "name": "SSO permission set with inline admin policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSO permission set with inline admin policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An inline policy granting Action \"*\" on Resource \"*\" in an SSO permission set gives every assigned user full admin across accounts. Scope the actions and resources.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-425",
              "name": "Secrets Manager version with hardcoded secret",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager version with hardcoded secret"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A secret_string written as a literal in Terraform config is stored in state, plan output, and version control — defeating the purpose of Secrets Manager. Generate it or reference it instead. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-426",
              "name": "SNS subscription over plaintext HTTP",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SNS subscription over plaintext HTTP"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An SNS subscription using the http protocol delivers message content unencrypted in transit. Use https.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnforce TLS 1.2+. Replace HTTP listeners with HTTPS, set a modern TLS policy (e.g. `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06`), and redirect HTTP→HTTPS. Disable weak ciphers and old protocol versions everywhere."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/elb-security-policy-table.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-427",
              "name": "SageMaker notebook with direct internet access",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SageMaker notebook with direct internet access"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A notebook instance with direct internet access can exfiltrate training data and download untrusted code. Disable direct internet access and route through a VPC instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-428",
              "name": "ElastiCache user with open access string",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ElastiCache user with open access string"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An ElastiCache user whose access string grants all commands on all keys (~* with +@all) is effectively an admin credential. Scope commands and key patterns to what the workload needs.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-431",
              "name": "Redshift Serverless workgroup publicly accessible",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redshift Serverless workgroup publicly accessible"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A publicly accessible Redshift Serverless workgroup exposes the data warehouse endpoint to the internet.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-432",
              "name": "Redshift Serverless namespace without customer-managed KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redshift Serverless namespace without customer-managed KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without kms_key_id the namespace uses the AWS-owned key; a customer-managed key is required for full control over encryption.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-433",
              "name": "VPC endpoint with public policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "VPC endpoint with public policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An absent policy means AWS applies the default endpoint policy, which allows full access to the service. An explicit policy granting a public principal has the same effect.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-434",
              "name": "Glue job without security configuration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Glue job without security configuration"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a security configuration the job lacks encryption for job bookmarks, CloudWatch logs, and S3 data.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-435",
              "name": "Timestream database without customer-managed KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Timestream database without customer-managed KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without kms_key_id the database uses the AWS-owned key; a customer-managed key is required for full control over encryption.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-436",
              "name": "RDS proxy without TLS enforcement",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS proxy without TLS enforcement"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without require_tls, clients may connect to the RDS proxy over plaintext, exposing credentials and queries on the network.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnforce TLS 1.2+. Replace HTTP listeners with HTTPS, set a modern TLS policy (e.g. `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06`), and redirect HTTP→HTTPS. Disable weak ciphers and old protocol versions everywhere."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/elb-security-policy-table.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-437",
              "name": "AppStream fleet with default internet access",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "AppStream fleet with default internet access"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "enable_default_internet_access gives streaming instances a public route to the internet, bypassing VPC egress controls.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-441",
              "name": "S3 access point policy allows public principal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "S3 access point policy allows public principal"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An Allow statement with Principal \"*\" on an S3 access point policy grants anonymous access through the access point.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-442",
              "name": "VPC endpoint service without acceptance required",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "VPC endpoint service without acceptance required"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without acceptance_required, any principal can create an endpoint against this service with no approval step.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-443",
              "name": "S3 bucket policy does not enforce TLS (SecureTransport)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "S3 bucket policy does not enforce TLS (SecureTransport)"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without an explicit Deny on aws:SecureTransport=false, bucket objects can be fetched over plaintext HTTP. Add a bucket policy statement denying s3:* when SecureTransport is false.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-444",
              "name": "EC2 instance with public IP address",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance with public IP address"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An instance with associate_public_ip_address=true is directly reachable from the internet. Use a load balancer or bastion instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-446",
              "name": "RDS instance without Multi-AZ",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without Multi-AZ"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Single-AZ RDS instances have no standby for failover. Enable multi_az for production databases.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable Multi-AZ for high availability and automatic failover:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  multi_az = true\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-501",
              "name": "IAM policy allows privilege escalation (create policy + attach policy)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy allows privilege escalation (create policy + attach policy)"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The policy combines a policy-authoring action (iam:CreatePolicy, iam:PutRolePolicy, iam:PutUserPolicy, iam:PutGroupPolicy) with a policy-attachment action (iam:AttachRolePolicy, iam:AttachUserPolicy, iam:AttachGroupPolicy). Attack path: create an administrative policy, attach it to your own role/user, assume full admin. Split authoring from attachment duties and scope each to least privilege.\n\nHow to fix:\nSplit policy authoring from attachment (this combination is the classic privilege\nescalation primitive). Remove `iam:CreatePolicy*` / `iam:Attach*Policy` from the\nworkload policy. Policy management belongs in a separate, human-gated CI/CD role —\nnever in an application runtime role."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html#grant-least-privilege",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-502",
              "name": "IAM policy allows privilege escalation via iam:PassRole on * with compute launch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy allows privilege escalation via iam:PassRole on * with compute launch"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The policy allows iam:PassRole on \"*\" together with a role-assumption primitive (ec2:RunInstances, lambda:CreateFunction, lambda:UpdateFunctionCode, ...). Attack path: launch a compute resource passing an administrative role, then steal its credentials from the instance metadata / environment. Scope iam:PassRole to specific role ARNs.\n\nHow to fix:\nScope `iam:PassRole` to the exact role ARNs the workload must pass, and only\ntogether with the specific compute-launch action it needs:\n{\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Action\": \"iam:PassRole\",\n  \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/AppExecutionRole\"\n}\nNever grant `iam:PassRole` on `*` — it lets the holder escalate to any role."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_use_passrole.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-503",
              "name": "IAM policy allows data exfiltration primitives",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy allows data exfiltration primitives"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The policy grants a known exfiltration pair: s3:GetObject + s3:PutObject on \"*\" (copy data to an attacker bucket), rds:CreateDBSnapshot + rds:ModifyDBSnapshotAttribute (share a snapshot cross-account), or ec2:CreateSnapshot + ec2:ModifySnapshotAttribute (share an EBS snapshot). Attack path: snapshot or copy production data, share it outside the account. Scope data-plane actions to specific resources.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-504",
              "name": "IAM permissions management actions allowed on all resources",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM permissions management actions allowed on all resources"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An Allow statement grants IAM write actions (iam:Put*, iam:Create*, iam:Update*, iam:Delete*, iam:Attach*, iam:Detach*, or iam:*) on Resource \"*\". Attack path: modify any identity's policies directly to escalate to admin. Read-only IAM actions (iam:Get*, iam:List*, iam:Generate*, iam:Simulate*) are excluded. Scope IAM management to specific users, roles, and policies.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-505",
              "name": "Write actions allowed on all resources",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Write actions allowed on all resources"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An Allow statement grants write actions (*:Put*, *:Create*, *:Update*, *:Delete*, *:Modify*) on Resource \"*\". Attack path: unconstrained writes let an attacker reconfigure, exfiltrate from, or destroy any resource in the account. The blanket Action \"*\" case is owned by AEGIS-AWS-006. Scope write actions to the resources the workload actually manages.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-506",
              "name": "Public load balancer without WAF",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Public load balancer without WAF"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An internet-facing load balancer with no WAFv2 web ACL association is exposed to common web exploits, bots, and application-layer DDoS with no managed filtering in front of it.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
