              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An AppSync GraphQL API with no WAFv2 web ACL association is exposed to injection, abuse, and denial-of-service traffic with no managed filtering in front of it.\n\nHow to fix:\nAttach a WAFv2 Web ACL with managed rule groups (AWSManagedRulesCommonRuleSet,\nAWSManagedRulesKnownBadInputsRuleSet) to the load balancer / API / distribution.\nSet the default action to block and monitor via CloudWatch metrics."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/waf/latest/developerguide/waf-chapter.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-514",
              "name": "ElastiCache replication group without auth token",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ElastiCache replication group without auth token"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When transit encryption is enabled on an ElastiCache replication group, an auth token is required for Redis AUTH. Without one, any client that can reach the endpoint authenticates trivially.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-515",
              "name": "ECR repository without KMS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ECR repository without KMS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ECR repositories default to AES256-managed encryption. A customer managed KMS key (encryption_type = KMS) gives key control and audit. Absent configuration defaults to AES256.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-516",
              "name": "EMR security configuration without in-transit encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EMR security configuration without in-transit encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "EMR clusters should encrypt data in transit between nodes. The security configuration JSON must set EnableInTransitEncryption to true. Absent or unparseable configuration is treated as undecidable.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-517",
              "name": "EMR security configuration without local disk encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EMR security configuration without local disk encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "EMR clusters should encrypt local disks at rest. The security configuration JSON must set EnableAtRestEncryption to true. Absent or unparseable configuration is treated as undecidable.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-518",
              "name": "DAX cluster without TLS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DAX cluster without TLS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "DAX clusters should use TLS for cluster endpoint encryption (cluster_endpoint_encryption_type = TLS). The provider default is unencrypted, so an absent value is a finding.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-519",
              "name": "Search domain with outdated TLS security policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Search domain with outdated TLS security policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domains should require at least TLS 1.2 (tls_security_policy of Policy-Min-TLS-1-2-2019-07 or newer). Older policies allow TLS 1.0/1.1.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-520",
              "name": "Transfer Server with outdated security policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Transfer Server with outdated security policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "AWS Transfer Family servers should use a security policy of TransferSecurityPolicy-2020-06 or newer. Older policies enable weak ciphers and key-exchange algorithms.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-521",
              "name": "ECR repository allows mutable image tags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ECR repository allows mutable image tags"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With image_tag_mutability = MUTABLE (the default when absent), an attacker who pushes to the repository can overwrite a trusted tag such as 'latest' with a malicious image. Set image_tag_mutability to IMMUTABLE so tags cannot be overwritten.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-528",
              "name": "Redshift cluster without enhanced VPC routing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Redshift cluster without enhanced VPC routing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Enhanced VPC routing forces all COPY/UNLOAD traffic between the Redshift cluster and data sources through the VPC, so VPC security groups, network ACLs and flow logs apply. It defaults to false; an absent attribute is therefore treated as disabled.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-534",
              "name": "Secrets in EC2 user_data",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets in EC2 user_data"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A secret (AWS key, password, token) embedded in EC2 user_data is visible in the console, API responses, and instance metadata. user_data_base64 is decoded before scanning. Pass secrets via instance profiles, SSM Parameter Store, or Secrets Manager instead. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove secrets from user_data immediately and rotate every exposed value.\nPass secrets via instance tags + Secrets Manager lookup at boot, or use an IAM\ninstance profile so the instance fetches secrets at runtime:\nuser_data = base64encode(templatefile(\"bootstrap.sh\", {}))  # no secrets inside\nNote: user_data is visible to anyone with EC2 DescribeInstances — treat any\nsecret ever placed there as compromised."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-535",
              "name": "Plaintext password in provisioner connection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Plaintext password in provisioner connection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A provisioner connection block with a hardcoded password stores credentials in Terraform state and version control. Use SSH keys, bastion hosts, or a secrets manager instead. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-536",
              "name": "Sensitive value exposed in Terraform output",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive value exposed in Terraform output"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Terraform output with a secret-like name (password, secret, key, token) that is not marked sensitive exposes the value in CLI output, state files, and CI logs. Set sensitive = true. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-537",
              "name": "Secret stored in resource tags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret stored in resource tags"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A resource tag with a secret-like key (password, secret, token) and a non-empty value exposes credentials in the console, APIs, and cost reports. Tags are not a secrets store. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-538",
              "name": "Secret in plaintext variable default",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret in plaintext variable default"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Terraform variable with a secret-like name (password, secret, token) and a hardcoded plaintext default bakes the credential into version control. Leave the default empty and inject via a secrets manager, vault provider, or CI secret. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-539",
              "name": "Secret in hardcoded local value",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret in hardcoded local value"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Terraform local with a secret-like name (password, secret, token) and a hardcoded string value bakes the credential into version control and state. Reference a secrets manager instead. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-540",
              "name": "RDS MySQL/Postgres instance without IAM database authentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS MySQL/Postgres instance without IAM database authentication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "MySQL and Postgres RDS instances support IAM database authentication, which replaces long-lived passwords with short-lived IAM credentials. With iam_database_authentication_enabled absent or false, access relies on static passwords. Engine-scoped: only fires for mysql/postgres engines; aurora and unknown engines are out of scope.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-541",
              "name": "Launch configuration allows IMDSv1",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Launch configuration allows IMDSv1"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without metadata_options.http_tokens = \"required\", instances launched from this configuration accept IMDSv1 requests, which SSRF vulnerabilities can abuse to steal credentials. AEGIS-AWS-204 covers aws_instance and aws_launch_template; this rule closes the gap for aws_launch_configuration.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-542",
              "name": "Lambda function not deployed in a VPC",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function not deployed in a VPC"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Lambda functions without a vpc_config block run outside your VPC, with no security-group or network-ACL control over their network access. Functions accessing VPC resources (RDS, ElastiCache) should declare vpc_config with subnet_ids and security_group_ids.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-543",
              "name": "RDS instance without CloudWatch log exports",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without CloudWatch log exports"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should export database logs to CloudWatch via enabled_cloudwatch_logs_exports for auditing and troubleshooting. Without log exports, forensic analysis after an incident is limited.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-544",
              "name": "RDS instance without auto minor version upgrades",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without auto minor version upgrades"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should enable auto_minor_version_upgrade so security patches are applied automatically during maintenance windows. Without it, the engine can drift behind on security fixes.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-546",
              "name": "RDS instance without copy_tags_to_snapshot",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without copy_tags_to_snapshot"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should copy tags to snapshots so backup resources inherit cost-allocation and ownership tags. Set copy_tags_to_snapshot = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-547",
              "name": "Lambda function without X-Ray active tracing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without X-Ray active tracing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Lambda functions should enable X-Ray active tracing for distributed request observability. Add a tracing_config block with mode = \"Active\".\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-548",
              "name": "Lambda function without reserved concurrent executions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without reserved concurrent executions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Lambda functions should set reserved_concurrent_executions to cap concurrency, preventing runaway scaling and unexpected cost. Set the attribute explicitly.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-551",
              "name": "ElastiCache replication group without Multi-AZ / failover",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ElastiCache replication group without Multi-AZ / failover"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: ElastiCache replication groups should enable Multi-AZ with automatic failover for high availability. Set multi_az_enabled = true and automatic_failover_enabled = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable Multi-AZ for high availability and automatic failover:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  multi_az = true\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-552",
              "name": "IAM access key created without PGP encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM access key created without PGP encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "IAM access keys should be created with pgp_key so the secret access key is encrypted at creation. Without it, the plaintext secret is stored in Terraform state. Set pgp_key to a PGP public key.\n\nHow to fix:\nRotate IAM access keys every 90 days or less. Prefer short-lived credentials:\nfederate via IAM Identity Center / SSO, or use instance profiles and ECS task roles\ninstead of long-lived keys. Audit with IAM Access Analyzer unused-access findings."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_credentials_access-keys.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-553",
              "name": "IAM policy uses NotAction with Allow effect",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy uses NotAction with Allow effect"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Combining \"NotAction\" with \"Effect\": \"Allow\" creates a deny-bypass pattern: the policy allows everything EXCEPT the listed actions, which is the inverse of least privilege. Rewrite using explicit Action lists.\n\nHow to fix:\nReplace `NotAction` + `Allow` with explicit `Action` lists. NotAction inverts\nmatching and is easy to misread — enumerate exactly what the identity may do\n(see AEGIS-AWS-006 fix)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_elements_notaction.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-554",
              "name": "IAM role with max session duration over 12 hours",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM role with max session duration over 12 hours"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "IAM roles should limit max_session_duration to 12 hours (43200 seconds) or less. Longer sessions increase the window for credential misuse if temporary credentials are compromised. Set max_session_duration \u003c= 43200.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-555",
              "name": "IAM group inline policy grants wildcard admin",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM group inline policy grants wildcard admin"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "IAM group inline policies should not grant \"Action\": \"*\" on \"Resource\": \"*\". Group membership changes silently expand the blast radius. Use managed policies with scoped permissions instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-556",
              "name": "Transit Gateway route table propagation enabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Transit Gateway route table propagation enabled"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Transit Gateway route table propagation automatically spreads routes from attachments, which can unintentionally expose VPCs to each other. Review each aws_ec2_transit_gateway_route_table_propagation and disable it where cross-VPC routing is not required.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-557",
              "name": "VPC endpoint policy allows all principals",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "VPC endpoint policy allows all principals"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "VPC endpoint policies with \"Principal\": \"*\" allow any AWS principal to use the endpoint, defeating the purpose of private connectivity. Scope the Principal to specific accounts or roles.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-562",
              "name": "KMS grant with wildcard operations",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "KMS grant with wildcard operations"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "KMS grants should list only the specific operations the grantee needs. Wildcard operations delegate full key control. Replace \"*\" with the minimal operation set.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-563",
              "name": "Secrets Manager secret without customer-managed KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager secret without customer-managed KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager secrets should use a customer-managed KMS key (kms_key_id) instead of the default AWS-managed key, so key rotation and access policies are under your control. Set kms_key_id.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-566",
              "name": "RDS Performance Insights without customer-managed KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS Performance Insights without customer-managed KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When Performance Insights is enabled, its data should be encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key rather than the default AWS key. Set performance_insights_kms_key_id.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-567",
              "name": "Security Hub not enabled in module",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security Hub not enabled in module"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "AWS Security Hub should be enabled for centralized security findings aggregation and CIS benchmark checks. Add an aws_securityhub_account resource. Only evaluated on modules with 5+ resources to avoid noise on trivial configurations.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable Security Hub with the CIS and FSBP standards:\nresource \"aws_securityhub_account\" \"this\" {}\nresource \"aws_securityhub_standards_subscription\" \"cis\" {\n  standards_arn = \"arn:aws:securityhub:::ruleset/cis-aws-foundations-benchmark/v/1.4.0\"\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-settingup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-568",
              "name": "CloudTrail trail without KMS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudTrail trail without KMS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "CloudTrail log files should be encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key so log integrity is under your control. Set kms_key_id on the aws_cloudtrail resource.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt CloudTrail logs with a KMS key (see AEGIS-AWS-336 fix):\nresource \"aws_cloudtrail\" \"this\" {\n  kms_key_id = aws_kms_key.cloudtrail.arn\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/encrypting-cloudtrail-log-files-with-aws-kms.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-569",
              "name": "Amazon Detective not enabled in module",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Amazon Detective not enabled in module"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Amazon Detective should be enabled for ML-driven security investigation across CloudTrail, VPC flow logs, and GuardDuty findings. Add an aws_detective_graph resource. Only evaluated on modules with 5+ resources to avoid noise on trivial configurations.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-601",
              "name": "S3 bucket without lifecycle configuration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "S3 bucket without lifecycle configuration"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: S3 buckets should define lifecycle rules to transition or expire objects, controlling long-term storage costs. Add a lifecycle_rule block or an aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration resource.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-604",
              "name": "Security group or rule without description",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security group or rule without description"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Security groups and their rules should carry descriptions so reviewers understand their purpose. Add a description attribute.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-605",
              "name": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should enable enhanced monitoring (monitoring_interval \u003e 0) for OS-level metrics that aid troubleshooting. Set monitoring_interval to 1, 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-607",
              "name": "RDS Postgres instance without query logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS Postgres instance without query logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS Postgres instances should export postgresql logs to CloudWatch for query auditing. Add \"postgresql\" to enabled_cloudwatch_logs_log_exports.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-608",
              "name": "RDS instance without Performance Insights",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without Performance Insights"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should enable Performance Insights for query-level performance analysis. Set performance_insights_enabled = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-609",
              "name": "Load balancer without access logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer without access logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Load balancers should log access to S3 for traffic analysis and incident investigation. Add an access_logs block with a bucket.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-610",
              "name": "CloudWatch log group without retention period",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudWatch log group without retention period"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: CloudWatch log groups should set retention_in_days so logs expire on a schedule instead of accumulating forever. Set retention_in_days explicitly.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-611",
              "name": "Neptune cluster without CloudWatch log exports",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Neptune cluster without CloudWatch log exports"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Neptune clusters should export audit logs to CloudWatch. Set enable_cloudwatch_logs_exports (e.g. [\"audit\"]).\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-618",
              "name": "Elasticsearch domain without audit logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Elasticsearch domain without audit logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domains should publish audit logs. Add a log_publishing_options block with log_type AUDIT_LOGS.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-623",
              "name": "Elasticsearch domain without any log publishing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Elasticsearch domain without any log publishing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domains should publish at least one log type (search, index, error or audit). Add a log_publishing_options block. (Audit-specific coverage: AEGIS-AWS-618.)\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-624",
              "name": "DocumentDB cluster without CloudWatch log exports",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DocumentDB cluster without CloudWatch log exports"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: DocumentDB clusters should export profiler and audit logs to CloudWatch. Set enabled_cloudwatch_logs_exports.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-630",
              "name": "IAM privilege chain: workload inherits sensitive actions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM privilege chain: workload inherits sensitive actions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A workload (EC2, Lambda) assumes an IAM role whose policy grants wildcard or sensitive actions. The chain traces role → policy → workload with evidence at each hop, showing the exact privilege inheritance path an attacker would abuse.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-634",
              "name": "RDS Cluster not encrypted with customer-managed KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS Cluster not encrypted with customer-managed KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An encrypted Aurora cluster with no kms_key_id uses the default AWS-managed key; a customer-managed key (CMK) gives control over key rotation, grants, and audit trail.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt the cluster with a customer-managed KMS key (see AEGIS-AWS-359 fix):\nresource \"aws_rds_cluster\" \"this\" {\n  storage_encrypted = true\n  kms_key_id        = aws_kms_key.rds.arn\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/Overview.Encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-637",
              "name": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domain without HTTPS enforcement",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domain without HTTPS enforcement"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A search domain whose domain_endpoint_options does not set enforce_https = true serves the cluster endpoint over plaintext HTTP, exposing credentials and query data in transit.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnforce TLS 1.2+. Replace HTTP listeners with HTTPS, set a modern TLS policy (e.g. `ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06`), and redirect HTTP→HTTPS. Disable weak ciphers and old protocol versions everywhere."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/elb-security-policy-table.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-641",
              "name": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domain without snapshot configuration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domain without snapshot configuration"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: a search domain without a snapshot_options block has no configured automated snapshot schedule, slowing recovery from data loss or corruption. Set automated_snapshot_start_hour.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-642",
              "name": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domain without slow logs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Elasticsearch/OpenSearch domain without slow logs"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: a search domain whose log_publishing_options publish neither SEARCH_SLOW_LOGS nor INDEX_SLOW_LOGS hides expensive queries, blinding performance tuning and abuse detection. Publish slow logs. (Audit-specific coverage: AEGIS-AWS-618; any-log coverage: AEGIS-AWS-623.)\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-645",
              "name": "Lambda function without code signing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without code signing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An aws_lambda_function without a code_signing_config block deploys code without signature validation, so tampered deployment packages run unchecked. Enforce code signing with AWS Signer.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-647",
              "name": "Lambda function without dead-letter queue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without dead-letter queue"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: an aws_lambda_function without a dead_letter_config block silently drops asynchronously-invoked events that fail all retries. Route failed events to an SQS queue or SNS topic.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-649",
              "name": "Lambda function with plaintext secret in environment variables",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function with plaintext secret in environment variables"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An aws_lambda_function environment variable holding a value that looks like a live credential (AWS key, 40-char secret, or long mixed token) exposes the secret to anyone with read access to the function configuration. Reference secrets from AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-657",
              "name": "IAM group uses an inline policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM group uses an inline policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The group embeds an inline policy document instead of attaching a managed policy. Inline policies cannot be reused, versioned, or reviewed centrally. Prefer aws_iam_policy with aws_iam_group_policy_attachment.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-658",
              "name": "IAM role uses an inline policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM role uses an inline policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The role embeds an inline policy document instead of attaching a managed policy. Inline policies cannot be reused, versioned, or reviewed centrally. Prefer aws_iam_policy with aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-659",
              "name": "IAM user uses an inline policy",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM user uses an inline policy"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The user embeds an inline policy document instead of attaching a managed policy. Inline policies cannot be reused, versioned, or reviewed centrally. Prefer aws_iam_policy with aws_iam_user_policy_attachment.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-661",
              "name": "IAM policy grants iam:PassRole on all resources",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy grants iam:PassRole on all resources"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An Allow statement grants iam:PassRole on Resource \"*\". PassRole lets the holder hand any role — including privileged ones — to an AWS service, a classic privilege-escalation step. Scope iam:PassRole to the specific role ARNs the workload needs.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-667",
              "name": "IAM role has no permissions boundary",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM role has no permissions boundary"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The role sets no permissions_boundary, so nothing caps the maximum permissions its policies can grant — a single overly broad policy attachment becomes fully effective. Set a permissions boundary as a guardrail.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-668",
              "name": "IAM access key secret not encrypted with pgp_key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM access key secret not encrypted with pgp_key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An active access key is created without pgp_key, so the secret access key is stored in plaintext in the Terraform state. Anyone with state access can read the long-lived credential. Set pgp_key to encrypt the secret.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-672",
              "name": "IAM user has no tags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM user has no tags"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The IAM user carries no tags, making ownership, purpose, and lifecycle untraceable in audits and complicating attribute-based access control. Tag identities with at least owner and purpose.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-673",
              "name": "IAM role has no description",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM role has no description"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The IAM role has no description, so reviewers cannot tell what the role is for or who owns it. Document the role's purpose in the description field.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-677",
              "name": "IAM policy statement has no Sid",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy statement has no Sid"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "At least one policy statement has no Sid, making the statement harder to reference in reviews, audit findings, and exception tracking. Give every statement a descriptive Sid.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-682",
              "name": "Launch template does not update its default version",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Launch template does not update its default version"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without update_default_version = true, new template versions never become the default, so instances and auto scaling groups silently keep launching from a stale version that may miss security fixes.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-683",
              "name": "EC2 instance without termination protection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without termination protection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "disable_api_termination is absent or false, so the instance can be terminated by an accidental or malicious API call. Enable it for instances that must not be deleted casually.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-684",
              "name": "EC2 instance without ebs_optimized declared",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without ebs_optimized declared"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ebs_optimized is absent, so the provider default (false) applies: the instance gets no dedicated EBS bandwidth. Declare ebs_optimized = true on EBS-backed workloads.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-691",
              "name": "Launch template without ebs_optimized declared",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Launch template without ebs_optimized declared"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ebs_optimized is absent from the launch template, so the provider default (false) applies to every instance launched from it: no dedicated EBS bandwidth. Declare ebs_optimized = true for EBS-backed workloads.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-692",
              "name": "EC2 instance without an IAM instance profile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without an IAM instance profile"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "iam_instance_profile is absent, so the instance has no IAM role: any AWS access must come from credentials baked into the image or user_data. Attach an instance profile with least-privilege permissions instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-696",
              "name": "EC2 root volume without encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 root volume without encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "root_block_device with encrypted absent or false leaves the instance's root volume unencrypted at rest, exposing the OS disk and any data written to it. Set encrypted = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-699",
              "name": "EC2 instance without Nitro Enclaves enabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without Nitro Enclaves enabled"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "enclave_options is absent, so Nitro Enclaves are unavailable on this instance. Workloads that process sensitive data should enable enclave_options { enabled = true } to isolate it.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-701",
              "name": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "monitoring_interval is absent or 0, so enhanced monitoring is disabled: OS-level metrics that catch host problems are missing. Set monitoring_interval between 1 and 60 seconds.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-702",
              "name": "RDS instance snapshots do not copy tags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance snapshots do not copy tags"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "copy_tags_to_snapshot is absent or false, so automated snapshots lose the instance's tags (owner, environment, cost center), which breaks tag-based access control and cost allocation on restores.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-706",
              "name": "RDS instance on the default option group",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance on the default option group"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "option_group_name is absent or a default group, so the instance runs with AWS's generic options (e.g. no OEM, no MariaDB audit plugin). Use a purpose-built option group.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-708",
              "name": "Aurora cluster on the default parameter group",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Aurora cluster on the default parameter group"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "db_cluster_parameter_group_name is absent or a default group, so cluster parameters (TLS enforcement, audit logging) stay at AWS defaults. Use a custom parameter group with hardened values.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-710",
              "name": "RDS master password not managed by Secrets Manager",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS master password not managed by Secrets Manager"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A plaintext master password is set without manage_master_user_password, so the credential sits in state and is never rotated automatically. Use manage_master_user_password = true so RDS manages rotation in Secrets Manager. The password value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
