              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-144",
              "name": "Storage account without infrastructure encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account without infrastructure encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without infrastructure_encryption_enabled = true encrypts data once at the service level. Infrastructure (double) encryption encrypts a second time at the hardware level with a different key. Enable it for defense in depth.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-145",
              "name": "Storage account allows cross-tenant replication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account allows cross-tenant replication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without allow_cross_tenant_replication = false permits object replication to storage accounts in other Microsoft Entra tenants, enabling data exfiltration paths. Disable cross-tenant replication.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-146",
              "name": "Storage account without blob versioning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account without blob versioning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account blob_properties without versioning_enabled = true keeps no prior blob versions, so ransomware or accidental overwrites cannot be rolled back. Enable blob versioning. (Soft delete: AEGIS-AZURE-126.)\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-147",
              "name": "Storage account without blob change feed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account without blob change feed"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account blob_properties without change_feed_enabled = true records no ordered log of blob create/update/delete events, blinding incident response to data tampering. Enable the blob change feed.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-148",
              "name": "Storage account with public network access (default)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account with public network access (default)"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without public_network_access_enabled = false exposes the storage endpoint to the internet by default. Disable public network access and use private endpoints instead. (Strict complement of AEGIS-AZURE-006, which covers an explicit true value.)\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-149",
              "name": "Storage account without network rules",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account without network rules"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without a network_rules block accepts storage traffic from any network by default. Add network_rules with default_action = \"Deny\" and explicit allow rules. (Strict complement of AEGIS-AZURE-021, which covers an explicit Allow default action.)\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-180",
              "name": "SQL server without managed identity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL server without managed identity"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_mssql_server without an identity block cannot use customer-managed keys for transparent data encryption or Entra ID authentication cleanly. Enable a system-assigned identity.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-181",
              "name": "Storage account without managed identity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account without managed identity"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without an identity block cannot use customer-managed keys stored in Key Vault. Enable a system-assigned identity when CMK encryption is required.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-191",
              "name": "Storage account with shared key access enabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account with shared key access enabled"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account with shared_access_key_enabled (the provider default) permits Shared Key authorization, which bypasses Entra ID and cannot be scoped. Disable it and use Entra ID with default_to_oauth_authentication.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-192",
              "name": "Storage account not defaulting to OAuth authentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account not defaulting to OAuth authentication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without default_to_oauth_authentication = true lets clients fall back to Shared Key auth. Default to Entra ID OAuth for data-plane operations.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-195",
              "name": "Storage account allows nested public items",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account allows nested public items"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account with allow_nested_items_to_be_public (the provider default) lets containers and blobs be marked public even when account-level public access is off. Disable it to enforce private-by-default.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-196",
              "name": "Storage account without infrastructure encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Storage account without infrastructure encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_storage_account without infrastructure_encryption_enabled = true encrypts data once at the service level. Enable infrastructure encryption for a second, platform-managed encryption layer.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-225",
              "name": "SQL server without Entra ID administrator",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL server without Entra ID administrator"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_mssql_server without an azuread_administrator block cannot use Entra ID authentication and relies on SQL logins. Configure an Entra ID admin and prefer Entra-only authentication.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AZURE-244",
              "name": "NSG inline security rule open to the internet",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "NSG inline security rule open to the internet"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "azurerm_network_security_group security_rule blocks with inbound Allow and a source of *, 0.0.0.0/0 or Internet expose the subnet to the world. Restrict inline rule sources.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-COMPOSE-001",
              "name": "Service runs privileged",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Service runs privileged"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "privileged: true disables all container isolation: the service gets every capability and full device access, so a compromise is a host compromise. Grant only the capabilities the service needs via cap_add.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-COMPOSE-002",
              "name": "Dangerous Linux capability granted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dangerous Linux capability granted"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The granted capability breaks container isolation: ALL grants everything, SYS_ADMIN is near-root, NET_ADMIN can reconfigure host networking, SYS_PTRACE can inspect host processes, and DAC_OVERRIDE bypasses file permissions. Grant only the minimal capabilities required.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-COMPOSE-005",
              "name": "Sensitive host path mounted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Sensitive host path mounted"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Mounting host system paths exposes host configuration, credentials, or the whole filesystem to the service. Mount only dedicated data directories, read-only where possible.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-COMPOSE-006",
              "name": "Secret in service environment",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret in service environment"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Literal secrets in the compose file are committed to version control and visible to anyone who can read the file or inspect the service. Reference secrets from a secrets manager or an env_file outside version control.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-COMPOSE-007",
              "name": "Service runs as root",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Service runs as root"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a user: directive the service runs as root; a container breakout then yields host root. Run as a non-root UID/GID.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable MFA on the account and restrict root usage. Create individual IAM users or federate via SSO, attach least-privilege policies, and never use root for daily operations."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_root-user.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-001",
              "name": "Base image uses :latest tag",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Base image uses :latest tag"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pinning :latest (or omitting the tag, which implies :latest) makes builds non-reproducible and can silently pull a new, vulnerable image. Pin an explicit version tag or digest.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-002",
              "name": "Container runs as root",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container runs as root"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a USER directive the container runs as root; an explicit USER root (or 0) is the same risk. A container breakout then yields host root. Switch to a non-root user.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable MFA on the account and restrict root usage. Create individual IAM users or federate via SSO, attach least-privilege policies, and never use root for daily operations."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_root-user.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-003",
              "name": "Secret in ENV directive",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret in ENV directive"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ENV values are baked into the image layers and visible to anyone who can pull the image (docker history, inspect). Inject secrets at runtime instead. The secret value is never shown in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-005",
              "name": "No HEALTHCHECK directive",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No HEALTHCHECK directive"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without a HEALTHCHECK the orchestrator cannot tell a wedged container from a healthy one, delaying restarts and hiding compromise-induced hangs.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-006",
              "name": "SSH port exposed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSH port exposed"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "EXPOSE 22 suggests SSH runs inside the container. Containers should be immutable and managed via the orchestrator, not SSH; an SSH daemon widens the attack surface.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-007",
              "name": "apt-get install without --no-install-recommends",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "apt-get install without --no-install-recommends"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Installing recommended packages bloats the image with unneeded software, enlarging the attack surface. Pass --no-install-recommends to apt-get install.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-008",
              "name": "apt-get update without cache cleanup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "apt-get update without cache cleanup"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "apt-get update leaves package lists in the image layer. Remove /var/lib/apt/lists/* in the same RUN to keep layers lean.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-011",
              "name": "Secret in ARG default value",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secret in ARG default value"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ARG defaults with secret-looking names are baked into the build cache and image history (docker history shows them). Pass secrets with BuildKit --secret mounts and multi-stage builds so they never land in a layer.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-015",
              "name": "No WORKDIR directive",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No WORKDIR directive"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without WORKDIR the build and the container run from /, so relative paths and stray files land in the filesystem root. Set an explicit WORKDIR for the application.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-017",
              "name": "No maintainer label",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No maintainer label"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Images without ownership metadata are harder to triage when a vulnerability is found. Add LABEL maintainer= or org.opencontainers.image.authors.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-018",
              "name": "No STOPSIGNAL directive",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No STOPSIGNAL directive"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without STOPSIGNAL the container gets SIGTERM, which apps that only handle SIGINT (or expect another signal) ignore until the orchestrator SIGKILLs them, risking data loss. Declare the signal your app handles for graceful shutdown.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-026",
              "name": "Full OS base image",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Full OS base image"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Full Linux distributions carry hundreds of packages the app never uses, each a potential CVE. Prefer minimal bases (distroless, Chainguard, Alpine, or -slim variants).\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-DOCKER-029",
              "name": "apt-get install without pinned versions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "apt-get install without pinned versions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Unpinned apt packages float across rebuilds, making images non-reproducible and silently pulling new versions. Pin versions: apt-get install pkg=1.2.3.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-GCP-001",
              "name": "GCS bucket publicly readable via IAM",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GCS bucket publicly readable via IAM"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A bucket IAM binding/member granting objectViewer or admin to allUsers or allAuthenticatedUsers exposes bucket data to the internet.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove `allUsers` / `allAuthenticatedUsers` from the bucket IAM policy. Grant\n`roles/storage.objectViewer` only to specific service accounts or groups:\nresource \"google_storage_bucket_iam_member\" \"viewer\" {\n  bucket = google_storage_bucket.this.name\n  role   = \"roles/storage.objectViewer\"\n  member = \"serviceAccount:app@project.iam.gserviceaccount.com\"\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/iam-roles",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "critical"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-GCP-002",
              "name": "Firewall rule open to the world",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Firewall rule open to the world"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A compute firewall with source_ranges containing 0.0.0.0/0 and an allow block permits traffic from the entire internet.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-GCP-007",
              "name": "Cloud SQL instance with public IP",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cloud SQL instance with public IP"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "google_sql_database_instance with settings.ip_configuration.ipv4_enabled = true exposes the database to the internet. Use private IP with VPC peering instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-GCP-016",
              "name": "GCS bucket without versioning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GCS bucket without versioning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "google_storage_bucket with versioning disabled or absent loses protection against accidental overwrites and deletions.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-GCP-019",
              "name": "Cloud SQL instance without deletion protection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cloud SQL instance without deletion protection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "google_sql_database_instance with deletion_protection = false (the provider default) can be destroyed accidentally. Enable deletion protection for production databases.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-GCP-025",
              "name": "GCS bucket without customer-managed encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GCS bucket without customer-managed encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "google_storage_bucket with no encryption block uses Google-managed keys. Customer-managed (CMEK) keys are required for regulated data.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
